<%--
Two_Factor_Challenge

The login challenge screen: the half-authenticated state between a correct password and a
correct second factor.

    <Two_Factor_Challenge $controller="Login_Controller" $method="verify_two_factor" />

WHY IT TAKES AN ENDPOINT. The framework will not verify the challenge for you - where a
signed-in user lands is application logic (a captured redirect, staff versus portal, an
interstitial the app requires), so the APPLICATION owns the verification endpoint and this
component posts to it. It is the <Rsx_Form> naming convention ($controller + $method) applied
to a control that is not a form over model fields.

THE ENDPOINT'S CONTRACT, both directions:
  it receives  {code: '123456'}  from the typed answer, or
               {assertion: {id, clientDataJSON, authenticatorData, signature}} from a passkey
  it calls     Rsx_Two_Factor::verify_challenge($params)
  it returns   {redirect: '/somewhere'} - followed with window.location
  it rejects   with a user-safe message, rendered inline here

ONE INPUT FOR TWO KINDS OF ANSWER. A 6-digit authenticator code and a recovery code both go
in the same box, because the server tries both and the user should not have to know which
kind of string they are holding. The box asks only for what this identity can answer with
(has_totp, has_recovery_codes from the challenge state):
    authenticator + recovery codes   "Authenticator or recovery code"
    authenticator only               "6-digit code"
    recovery codes only              "Recovery code"
    neither (a passkey only)         no box and no Verify - the passkey button is the answer
$placeholder replaces the derived text (the box still appears only when there is a code to
type).

THE THROTTLE MESSAGE MUST SURVIVE. Auth_Throttled_Exception surfaces as an ordinary Ajax
rejection, so it lands in the same inline error as a wrong code. That is deliberate: "you are
doing that too fast" is the most important thing this screen ever says, and a component that
only rendered messages it recognised would swallow it.

STATES, in the order the template tests them:
  data.challenge === null pending   the first load has not answered -> a loading notice
  data.challenge === null           nothing is pending              -> a notice; 'no_challenge' fires
  otherwise                         the masked address and the answer controls

THE ADDRESS is the masked one (c***e@example.com) unless the application sets
rsx.two_factor.challenge_shows_full_email, in which case the challenge state carries the full
address and this prints it.

CANCEL. With $cancel_url the screen offers Cancel beside Verify: it discards the pending
challenge (challenge_abandon -> abandon_challenge()) and navigates to $cancel_url. The two
then sit in a row of their own beneath the box, centred, Cancel first - an input, Verify and
Cancel do not fit one row. Without $cancel_url nothing changes: Verify sits beside the box.

Args:
  $controller  (required) - the application controller holding the verification endpoint.
  $method      (required) - the endpoint's method name.
  $cancel_url  - where Cancel leads (normally the login page). Absent: no Cancel.
  $placeholder - the code box's placeholder, replacing the derived one (e.g. '------').

Events:
  no_challenge - nothing is pending: an expired window, or a direct visit. The host page
                 sends the visitor back to the login screen.

See: php artisan rsx:man two_factor
--%>
<Define:Two_Factor_Challenge tag="div">
    <% if (!this.args.controller || !this.args.method) { throw new Error('Two_Factor_Challenge requires $controller and $method'); } %>

    <% if (!this.data.loaded) { %>
        <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__notice">Checking your sign-in...</div>
    <% } else if (!this.data.challenge) { %>
        <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__notice">This sign-in is no longer waiting for a second step.</div>
    <% } else { %>
        <%
            const _c = this.data.challenge;
            const _code_answer = _c.has_totp || _c.has_recovery_codes;
            const _passkey = _c.has_passkey && this.state.passkeys_supported;
            const _cancel = !!this.args.cancel_url;
        %>
        <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__body">
            <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__identity">
                Signing in as <span class="Two_Factor_Challenge__email"><%= _c.email || _c.email_masked %></span>
            </div>

            <% if (_code_answer && _cancel) { %>
                <input type="text" class="form-control Two_Factor_Challenge__code-input" $sid="code"
                       inputmode="<%= _c.has_recovery_codes ? 'text' : 'numeric' %>" autocomplete="one-time-code" autofocus
                       placeholder="<%= this.placeholder() %>" aria-label="Verification code" />
            <% } else if (_code_answer) { %>
                <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__answer">
                    <input type="text" class="form-control Two_Factor_Challenge__code-input" $sid="code"
                           inputmode="<%= _c.has_recovery_codes ? 'text' : 'numeric' %>" autocomplete="one-time-code" autofocus
                           placeholder="<%= this.placeholder() %>" aria-label="Verification code" />
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" $sid="verify">Verify</button>
                </div>
            <% } else if (!_passkey) { %>
                <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__notice">This account's second step is a passkey, and this browser cannot use one.</div>
            <% } %>

            <% if (_cancel) { %>
                <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__actions">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" $sid="cancel">Cancel</button>
                    <% if (_code_answer) { %>
                        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" $sid="verify">Verify</button>
                    <% } %>
                </div>
            <% } %>

            <% if (_passkey) { %>
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary Two_Factor_Challenge__passkey" $sid="passkey">
                    Use passkey
                </button>
            <% } %>

            <% if (this.state.error) { %>
                <div class="Two_Factor_Challenge__error"><%= this.state.error %></div>
            <% } %>
        </div>
    <% } %>
</Define:Two_Factor_Challenge>
